a family in California is calling on  tech companies to do more to protect the  consumer after their home security  system was apparently hijacked Laura  Lyons says that hackers took control of  her nest security camera she says a  mysterious voice warned of an incoming  missile attack from North Korea the  announcement we're glad to say turned  out to be a hoax but Lian says her  family was terrified Anna Warner shows  us what you can do to keep your devices  in your data safe from hackers and a  good morning good morning tech companies  like Google Apple and Facebook offer a  security feature called two-factor  authentication it makes you take an  extra step such as entering a code to  prove it's really you after giving your  username and password it's something  Laura Lyons did not have turned on until  this happened if we'd had any inkling  that a data breach had occurred when we  heard it coming out of that camera we  would have instantly been suspect as  opposed to several minutes of quite  frankly sheer terror those several  minutes of terror Laura Lyons says began  when the speaker on her nest security  camera began blasting an emergency alert  through her family's California home she  says what followed was a voice warning  them that three missiles from North  Korea were headed to the US and to take  shelter the hoax sent Lyons her husband  and their eight-year-old son into a  panic  we called nest they admitted that they  had received multiple reports of nest  cameras being hacked in the last week in  a statement to CBS this morning Google  which owns nest says nest was not  breached instead the company says  customers like lions had been using  compromised passwords exposed through  breaches on other websites last week a  website that tracks compromised online  logins claims it uncovered over 790  million unique email addresses and  passwords all compiled from more than  2,000 alleged databases it doesn't just  see incredibly well it's got serious  brain power google says it recently  reset all nest accounts where customers  reused passwords that were previously  exposed and  is actively introducing features that  will reject compromised passwords you  don't want to say anything is a hundred  percent secure all the time but wired  news editor Brian Barrett says while  tech companies stress greater security  and convenience users shouldn't lose  sight of taking precautions of their own  incidents like this remind people that  they need to to give their devices a  second thought and take a strong look at  if they really need them why they need  them and if they're going to get them  how they're going to make sure that they  stay protected in addition to not  reusing passwords and enabling  two-factor authentication Barrett also  recommends a password manager Laura  Lyons says since the incident involving  her family's nest security camera her  husband changed their passwords turned  on two-factor authentication and turned  off the devices microphone and speaker  Norah that's a very good advice thank  you
 

